# =============================================================================
# a1-probe-fix.patch — the Phase 8 Wave-0 A1 probe, repaired
#
# APPLIES TO: packages/backend/src/index.ts AND packages/backend/src/mcp-server-spec.ts
#   AS THEY EXIST AT COMMIT 68199fa
#   ("feat(08-01): add the temporary A1 probe to the diagnostics block").
#   The second file was added 2026-08-28 by plan 08-17 and is a COMPATIBILITY
#   edit only — see section 5.
#   It does NOT apply to HEAD, deliberately — the probe is not in HEAD and must
#   not be put there. See NEVER COMMIT THIS TO HEAD below.
#
# THE DEFECT IT FIXES, IN ONE SENTENCE: the probe decided grandchild liveness
#   with `signalRef.process?.kill?.(pid, 0) ?? false` on a runtime whose own
#   diagnostics field, five lines earlier in the same report, measured
#   `process.kill` as `undefined` — so the optional chain yielded `undefined`,
#   `?? false` made `alive === false`, and the favourable verdict string
#   "grandchild-died (detached honoured)" was emitted UNCONDITIONALLY. The
#   unfavourable string was unreachable. The 2026-08-27 A1 reading therefore
#   carried no information and was RETRACTED on 2026-08-28.
#
# THE SECOND DEFECT, which is a real process leak rather than a tidy-up: the
#   fixture cleanup loop at the foot of the probe signalled its two fixture pids
#   through that SAME absent primitive, so on the shipping sandbox it was a
#   silent no-op. The 2026-08-27 run left its fixture processes running, and
#   every further diagnostics call would have leaked two more.
#
# THE THREE-VALUED CONTRACT. The patched probe reports exactly THREE outcomes,
#   and the third is a first-class result rather than an error or a retry:
#
#     grandchild-survived (detached NOT honoured)   A1 is FALSIFIED
#     grandchild-died (detached honoured)           A1 is CONFIRMED
#     inconclusive: <reason> — the probe could not tell whether the target is
#                              still in the process table
#
#   The first two strings are BYTE-IDENTICAL to what the 2026-08-27 run
#   produced, on purpose: a re-run must be comparable with the withdrawn
#   reading. The third shares no verdict word with either — no "died", no
#   "survived", no "honoured" — so a reader skimming a diagnostics report cannot
#   mistake a non-answer for either outcome. RECORD IT VERBATIM. Do not retry
#   it, and do not read it as either verdict; that coalescing is the exact bug
#   this patch removes.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES — three things, and only these three:
#   1. The verdict determination, which now spawns a process-table enumerator
#      (`buildLivenessProbePlan`), classifies its outcome
#      (`classifyLivenessObservation`) and renders it (`formatSpikeVerdict`) —
#      all three from `packages/backend/src/kill-plan.ts` at HEAD, each proven
#      by executed unit cases including a 120-combination totality case. The
#      probe COMPOSES them rather than re-deriving the logic.
#   2. The fixture cleanup, which now spawns `buildOrphanKillPlan`'s positive
#      single-pid killer per fixture pid, unconditionally, on every exit path
#      including the inconclusive and error arms.
#   3. The now-unused callable `process.kill` reference, removed so no future
#      edit reaches for it again. `spikeProcessKillType` still REPORTS the
#      `typeof`, because that reading is what exposed the defect.
#
# 4. TWO COMPATIBILITY EDITS, NAMED RATHER THAN SMUGGLED IN. They are not probe
#   changes and they alter no behaviour; they exist because HEAD's `platform.ts`
#   has to travel with the patch (HEAD's `kill-plan.ts` imports
#   `resolveWindowsSystemBinary` from it, which the historical `platform.ts`
#   does not export) and HEAD's `selectComspec` / `getWhichCommand` take
#   `systemRootFallback` as a REQUIRED member, so every call site must state an
#   answer (G-01). Both historical call sites are given `systemRootFallback: ""`,
#   which `selectComspec`'s own comment records as falling through to exactly the
#   pre-G-01 last-resort behaviour this commit already had. Both are win32-only
#   paths and the probe returns early on win32, so they are moot for the
#   measurement either way. They were discovered by RUNNING the control, not by
#   reasoning about it: the first run of `verify-a1-patch.sh` failed step 3 with
#   these two TS2345 errors, which is the control doing its job.
#
# 5. SIX MORE COMPATIBILITY EDITS, ADDED 2026-08-28 BY PLAN 08-17, and named here
#   for the same reason section 4 names its two: they are not probe changes and
#   they must not be discovered later as smuggled-in behaviour.
#
#   HOW THEY WERE FOUND: by RUNNING `verify-a1-patch.sh`, which failed step 3
#   with four TS2345 errors. That is the standing control doing exactly the job
#   its own header predicts — "a signature change at HEAD that the patch's call
#   sites no longer satisfy, and `tsc --noEmit` fails here rather than on the
#   maintainer's machine an hour into a hardware session." The patch's previous
#   VERIFIED line was true on 2026-08-28 and became false the same day.
#
#   WHAT MOVED: commit 39876b5 ("give the spawned provider CLI a POSIX identity
#   when process.env is empty") made `identityFallback` a REQUIRED member of
#   `buildSpawnEnv` in `platform.ts` — the same G-01 discipline as
#   `systemRootFallback`, so every call site must state an answer. HEAD's
#   `platform.ts` travels with this patch, so the four historical call sites
#   (three in index.ts, one in mcp-server-spec.ts) stopped compiling.
#
#   THE SIX EDITS: the `./platform` import gains `derivePosixIdentity` and
#   `type PosixIdentityFallback`; `getPosixIdentityFallback` is reproduced
#   byte-for-byte from HEAD beside the `getKnownHomeDirs` this commit already
#   has; and the four call sites each state an answer.
#
#   THE ANSWER IS NOT THE SAME AT ALL FOUR SITES, AND THE SPLIT IS THE POINT.
#   `mcp-server-spec.ts` gets `{ platform: undefined, homeDir: undefined }`,
#   which makes `derivePosixIdentity` return `{}` and reproduces 68199fa byte for
#   byte — the MCP server child authenticates to Caido with a token Drift injects
#   and never through the macOS Keychain, so it needs no USER. The three
#   `index.ts` sites get the real derived floor, and the load-bearing one is the
#   PROVIDER CLI SPAWN. Caido's sandbox exposes an EMPTY `process.env`; at this
#   commit that leaves the provider CLI with no USER, and `env -i claude -p`
#   answers "Not logged in" because the credential is in the Keychain keyed on
#   the user name. Without the floor NO TURN CAN START on a real install, so the
#   pre-fix Control — a `pgrep -f mcp-server.mjs` count taken DURING a live turn
#   — cannot be taken at all. Plan 08-16 hit that wall on abfbc17 and recorded it.
#
#   IT DOES NOT CONTAMINATE THE CONTROL, and this is the claim to check rather
#   than accept. The floor adds three environment keys to a spawned child. It
#   touches no termination path: no group kill, no signal ladder, and no orphan
#   reap — there is no reap at this commit, which is precisely what makes the
#   build a Control. It also makes the before/after pair differ in ONE variable
#   instead of two, because the post-fix reading of 2026-08-28 was itself taken on
#   a build carrying this floor.
#
#   IF YOU WANT THE OTHER ANSWER: replacing `getPosixIdentityFallback()` with
#   `{ platform: undefined, homeDir: undefined }` at the three index.ts sites
#   reproduces 68199fa exactly and still yields the A1 reading, because the probe
#   spawns `node` fixtures that need no identity. It costs the Control.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE: the fixture sources, the detached spawn, the group
#   kill via the OS `kill` utility, the synchronous-throw guard around the
#   fixture spawn (a probe that rejects the diagnostics RPC is worse than one
#   that reports inconclusive), the win32 early return, or the three
#   diagnostics field names.
#
# NEVER COMMIT THIS TO HEAD. The probe spawns two fixture node processes on
#   EVERY diagnostics call. Opening Settings repeatedly on a machine holding a
#   live Caido session token is an unbounded process spawn, which is threat
#   T-08-76. It exists as a patch file applied to a checked-out historical
#   commit for a LOCAL, UNCOMMITTED, maintainer-driven build, and for nothing
#   else. It is never signed and never released.
#
# HOW TO APPLY, AND HOW TO RESTORE. `verify-a1-patch.sh` beside this file does
#   the whole thing in a scratch worktree that never touches your working tree,
#   and is the control that proves the patch still applies and still builds.
#   The by-hand form, for a maintainer who wants the built plugin on their own
#   machine, is in `08-SPIKE.md` § How to run this spike later. The one-line
#   restore, whatever went wrong:
#
#     git checkout -- packages/backend/src/index.ts packages/backend/src/mcp-server-spec.ts
#
# VERIFIED: applied, type-checked and built on 2026-08-28 by plan 08-15, via
#   `bash .planning/phases/08-process-lifecycle/verify-a1-patch.sh` (exit 0).
#
#   THAT LINE WENT STALE THE SAME DAY, and the staleness is preserved above
